vsp and 12 sprawl, matching the report.\",\"observed_value\":{\"rows\":24,\"unique_block_ids\":24,\"vsp_rows\":12,\"sprawl_rows\":12},\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":1,\"end_line\":25,\"quoted_text\":\"block_id,trellis_system,vine_age_years,canopy_leaf_layers,anthocyanin_mg_per_g\\nBLK-01,vsp,14,1.3,1.42\\nBLK-02,sprawl,12,2.4,1.18\\nBLK-03,vsp,18,1.1,1.58\\nBLK-04,sprawl,16,2.7,1.35\\nBLK-05,vsp,9,1.6,1.31\\nBLK-06,sprawl,11,3.0,1.09\\nBLK-07,vsp,22,0.9,1.67\\nBLK-08,sprawl,20,2.2,1.41\\nBLK-09,vsp,15,1.4,1.49\\nBLK-10,sprawl,13,2.6,1.26\\nBLK-11,vsp,25,0.8,1.75\\nBLK-12,sprawl,24,3.1,1.12\\nBLK-13,vsp,10,1.7,1.38\\nBLK-14,sprawl,17,2.3,1.33\\nBLK-15,vsp,19,1.2,1.61\\nBLK-16,sprawl,8,2.8,1.21\\nBLK-17,vsp,16,1.3,1.53\\nBLK-18,sprawl,21,3.2,1.05\\nBLK-19,vsp,12,1.5,1.44\\nBLK-20,sprawl,19,2.1,1.39\\nBLK-21,vsp,23,1.0,1.70\\nBLK-22,sprawl,15,2.5,1.28\\nBLK-23,vsp,11,1.8,1.29\\nBLK-24,sprawl,9,2.9,1.16\"}]},{\"description\":\"The workflow explicitly enforces uniqueness of the independent-unit column before any analysis: it raises a ValueError if block_id does not uniquely identify each row, so a repeated unit could not silently enter the Welch t-test.\",\"observed_value\":\"uniqueness guard present and would abort on duplicate block_id\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":35,\"end_line\":37,\"quoted_text\":\"    identifiers = [block[UNIT_COLUMN] for block in blocks]\\n    if len(set(identifiers)) != len(identifiers):\\n        raise ValueError(UNIT_COLUMN + \\\" does not uniquely identify a row\\\")\"},{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":21,\"end_line\":21,\"quoted_text\":\"UNIT_COLUMN = \\\"block_id\\\"\"}]},{\"description\":\"The data description states that the file is already at the independent-unit level: one row per independently owned and managed block, with block_id named as the independent unit column, and each code appearing on exactly one row.\",\"observed_value\":\"row = one block; block_id declared as independent unit; one code per row\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":21,\"end_line\":22,\"quoted_text\":\"One row is: one independently owned and managed Syrah vineyard block, summarized by the single 100-berry composite sample collected in that block on its harvest day\\nIndependent unit column: block_id\"},{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":26,\"end_line\":27,\"quoted_text\":\"- `block_id`: the survey code for the vineyard block, for example BLK-07. Each\\n  code appears on exactly one row; there are as many rows as blocks.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The report's selected result is a Welch two-sample t-test on the 24 block-level values, and its claim that rows correspond one-to-one with independent blocks matches the data. The reported group means (1.514 and 1.236 mg/g, n = 12 each) reproduce from the raw column values.\",\"observed_value\":{\"vsp_mean_recomputed\":1.514,\"sprawl_mean_recomputed\":1.236},\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":5,\"end_line\":5,\"quoted_text\":\"The file records 24 Syrah vineyard blocks, each at a different estate and each trained on a single trellis system. Each block contributes exactly one analyzed row: a 100-berry composite drawn across the block at harvest and assayed once. Rows therefore correspond one-to-one with independent blocks, and no block appears twice.\"},{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":18,\"end_line\":18,\"quoted_text\":\"[selected-result] Welch two-sample t-test on 24 independent vineyard blocks (12 vertical shoot positioning, 12 sprawl): mean berry skin anthocyanin was 1.514 mg/g under vertical shoot positioning versus 1.236 mg/g under sprawl, a difference of 0.278 mg/g (t = 4.99, df = 20.86, p < 0.001); anthocyanin was higher under vertical shoot positioning.\"}]}],\"counterevidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The closest candidate for repeated unit entry is that 100 berries were collected per block, which could in principle have yielded many sub-block measurements per unit. However, the description states the berries were pooled into a single composite before extraction, producing exactly one anthocyanin value per block, so no within-block replicates ever enter the row-independent procedure.\",\"observed_value\":\"100 berries per block pooled to a single composite value before assay\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":15,\"end_line\":18,\"quoted_text\":\"picked. A crew walked a zigzag transect and collected 100 berries spread across\\nthe block; those berries were pooled into a single composite before the skins\\nwere ever extracted, so the laboratory produced one anthocyanin number per\\nblock rather than many.\"}]}],\"falsification_attempt\":\"I tried to demonstrate the issue by (1) scanning all 24 block_id values in data/input.csv for duplicates — none exist, and every identifier BLK-01 through BLK-24 appears exactly once; (2) checking whether the workflow could aggregate or expand rows so that a block contributes more than one value to the t-test — collect() pulls exactly one anthocyanin value per row and read_blocks() aborts on duplicate block_id, so it cannot; (3) looking for sub-unit replication (multiple berries or point-quadrat insertions per block) entering the test — both were pooled or averaged to a single block-level value in the field before the file was created; and (4) recomputing group counts and means from the raw column, which match the report (12/12, 1.514 vs 1.236 mg/g). No path by which an independent unit enters the procedure more than once was found.\",\"cross_case_evidence_used\":false,\"unresolved_material_questions\":[],\"self_reported_confidence\":\"high\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-i2-reviewer-fable-42","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:1ac3fe6951cd6072e49f","verdict":"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope","bounded_statement":null,"root_cause":null,"issue_class":null,"evidence_atoms":[{"description":"All 24 data rows in data/input.csv carry distinct block_id values (BLK-01 through BLK-24), so the declared independent unit appears exactly once per row and no unit enters the two-sample test more than once. Group counts are 12 vsp and 12 sprawl, matching the report.","observed_value":{"rows":24,"unique_block_ids":24,"vsp_rows":12,"sprawl_rows":12},"source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":1,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"block_id,trellis_system,vine_age_years,canopy_leaf_layers,anthocyanin_mg_per_g\nBLK-01,vsp,14,1.3,1.42\nBLK-02,sprawl,12,2.4,1.18\nBLK-03,vsp,18,1.1,1.58\nBLK-04,sprawl,16,2.7,1.35\nBLK-05,vsp,9,1.6,1.31\nBLK-06,sprawl,11,3.0,1.09\nBLK-07,vsp,22,0.9,1.67\nBLK-08,sprawl,20,2.2,1.41\nBLK-09,vsp,15,1.4,1.49\nBLK-10,sprawl,13,2.6,1.26\nBLK-11,vsp,25,0.8,1.75\nBLK-12,sprawl,24,3.1,1.12\nBLK-13,vsp,10,1.7,1.38\nBLK-14,sprawl,17,2.3,1.33\nBLK-15,vsp,19,1.2,1.61\nBLK-16,sprawl,8,2.8,1.21\nBLK-17,vsp,16,1.3,1.53\nBLK-18,sprawl,21,3.2,1.05\nBLK-19,vsp,12,1.5,1.44\nBLK-20,sprawl,19,2.1,1.39\nBLK-21,vsp,23,1.0,1.70\nBLK-22,sprawl,15,2.5,1.28\nBLK-23,vsp,11,1.8,1.29\nBLK-24,sprawl,9,2.9,1.16"}]},{"description":"The workflow explicitly enforces uniqueness of the independent-unit column before any analysis: it raises a ValueError if block_id does not uniquely identify each row, so a repeated unit could not silently enter the Welch t-test.","observed_value":"uniqueness guard present and would abort on duplicate block_id","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":35,"end_line":37,"quoted_text":"    identifiers = [block[UNIT_COLUMN] for block in blocks]\n    if len(set(identifiers)) != len(identifiers):\n        raise ValueError(UNIT_COLUMN + \" does not uniquely identify a row\")"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":21,"end_line":21,"quoted_text":"UNIT_COLUMN = \"block_id\""}]},{"description":"The data description states that the file is already at the independent-unit level: one row per independently owned and managed block, with block_id named as the independent unit column, and each code appearing on exactly one row.","observed_value":"row = one block; block_id declared as independent unit; one code per row","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":21,"end_line":22,"quoted_text":"One row is: one independently owned and managed Syrah vineyard block, summarized by the single 100-berry composite sample collected in that block on its harvest day\nIndependent unit column: block_id"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":26,"end_line":27,"quoted_text":"- `block_id`: the survey code for the vineyard block, for example BLK-07. Each\n  code appears on exactly one row; there are as many rows as blocks."}]},{"description":"The report's selected result is a Welch two-sample t-test on the 24 block-level values, and its claim that rows correspond one-to-one with independent blocks matches the data. The reported group means (1.514 and 1.236 mg/g, n = 12 each) reproduce from the raw column values.","observed_value":{"vsp_mean_recomputed":1.514,"sprawl_mean_recomputed":1.236},"source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":5,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"The file records 24 Syrah vineyard blocks, each at a different estate and each trained on a single trellis system. Each block contributes exactly one analyzed row: a 100-berry composite drawn across the block at harvest and assayed once. Rows therefore correspond one-to-one with independent blocks, and no block appears twice."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":18,"end_line":18,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Welch two-sample t-test on 24 independent vineyard blocks (12 vertical shoot positioning, 12 sprawl): mean berry skin anthocyanin was 1.514 mg/g under vertical shoot positioning versus 1.236 mg/g under sprawl, a difference of 0.278 mg/g (t = 4.99, df = 20.86, p < 0.001); anthocyanin was higher under vertical shoot positioning."}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The closest candidate for repeated unit entry is that 100 berries were collected per block, which could in principle have yielded many sub-block measurements per unit. However, the description states the berries were pooled into a single composite before extraction, producing exactly one anthocyanin value per block, so no within-block replicates ever enter the row-independent procedure.","observed_value":"100 berries per block pooled to a single composite value before assay","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":15,"end_line":18,"quoted_text":"picked. A crew walked a zigzag transect and collected 100 berries spread across\nthe block; those berries were pooled into a single composite before the skins\nwere ever extracted, so the laboratory produced one anthocyanin number per\nblock rather than many."}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to demonstrate the issue by (1) scanning all 24 block_id values in data/input.csv for duplicates — none exist, and every identifier BLK-01 through BLK-24 appears exactly once; (2) checking whether the workflow could aggregate or expand rows so that a block contributes more than one value to the t-test — collect() pulls exactly one anthocyanin value per row and read_blocks() aborts on duplicate block_id, so it cannot; (3) looking for sub-unit replication (multiple berries or point-quadrat insertions per block) entering the test — both were pooled or averaged to a single block-level value in the field before the file was created; and (4) recomputing group counts and means from the raw column, which match the report (12/12, 1.514 vs 1.236 mg/g). No path by which an independent unit enters the procedure more than once was found.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]},"ttft_ms":32873,"ttft_stream_ms":2220,"time_to_request_ms":17,"type":"result","duration_ms":63246,"uuid":"6b6998d7-d3a0-4d61-8c4d-30e187efaee1"}
